{"A":"Establish a strong sequential baseline: replace recursive randomized quicksort with a standards-compliant optimized sort (libc qsort and/or an inlined introsort), preserving generation, byte representation, MD5 output, and all positive N. Measure correctness and timing; retain only a real win over 70.15 ms.","B":"Research integer-specific sorting for the generated nonnegative int domain: implement and benchmark a buffered LSD radix/counting approach with safe allocation and exact ascending order. Compare pass widths and serial performance on ARM64; do not hardcode checksums or case sizes.","C":"Research a scalable four-core alternative independently: OpenMP parallel radix histogram/scatter or parallel merge sort, with thresholds that avoid small-input overhead, race-free deterministic output, and portable fallback behavior. Validate every harness case and report timing versus sequential candidates.","G":"Integrate only the best measured valid program from A/B/C. Rebuild and remeasure each candidate and any proposed hybrid independently on the ARM64 4-core target; verify MD5/correctness for all cases, preserve input/output contract, and select the fastest measured result rather than assuming optimizations combine.","analysis":"The sole hot path is deterministic array generation followed by an inefficient recursive randomized quicksort. Baseline is correct but already only 70.154 ms on 4 ARM64 cores, so low-overhead O(N) integer sorting and optimized sequential sorting are primary opportunities; parallel work must overcome synchronization and memory-bandwidth costs. Output correctness depends on a genuinely sorted full array before MD5, not merely an expected digest."}